# Where exactly the jaws close, planned from the depth image. Metres, radians,
# seconds, <robot_name>/base_link.
#
# Served by world_model_node's ~plan_grasp (grasp_planner.py + footprint.py,
# ported from rework-attempt-1's standalone grasp_planner_node — folded in
# here instead of as its own process, per the RAM lesson from that rework:
# same capability, no extra process). task_orchestrator (the other half of
# world_model_node) calls it once per pre-grasp attempt and falls back to
# grasp_geometry.baseline_plan() when it declines — see
# TaskOrchestrator._choose_plan. A caller with nothing behind this name yet
# (startup ordering, or the service down) degrades the same way: the
# baseline runs exactly as if this service didn't exist.
float64[4] bbox                  # the target's detection box, PIXELS [x1, y1, x2, y2]
time stamp                       # the camera frame the box was detected in
geometry_msgs/Point position     # the world model's estimate of the target's centre
geometry_msgs/Vector3 size       # x = depth, y = width, z = height; all 0 = unknown
float64 table_z                  # table surface height
float64 max_axis_deviation       # how far the closing axis may turn from across-the-approach
---
bool planned                     # false = no plan, keep the baseline (reason says why)
string reason
GraspPlan plan
geometry_msgs/Point contact_left
geometry_msgs/Point contact_right
float64 width                    # the object's width across the jaws at the grip height
float64 seat_turn                # how far the object will turn in the closing jaws
int32 points                     # depth points on the target
float64 frame_offset             # seconds between the requested stamp and the depth frame used
float64 table_z                  # the surface the target stands on, MEASURED from this depth frame
                                 # (NaN = could not be measured: the request's table_z stands)
